You can achieve that using either local storage, session storage, or cookies in JavaScript. Here’s a quick rundown of these methods:

Local Storage
Data is stored with no expiration time. It will be available even after the browser is closed.

Saving a variable
javascript
localStorage.setItem('variableName', variableValue);
Retrieving a variable
javascript
let variableValue = localStorage.getItem('variableName');
Session Storage
Data is stored for the duration of the page session. It’s cleared when the page session ends.

Saving a variable
javascript
sessionStorage.setItem('variableName', variableValue);
Retrieving a variable
javascript
let variableValue = sessionStorage.getItem('variableName');
Cookies
Data stored in cookies can have an expiration date. It's often used for session management.

Saving a variable
javascript
document.cookie = "variableName=" + variableValue + "; path=/";
Retrieving a variable
javascript
function getCookie(name) {
    let match = document.cookie.match(new RegExp('(^| )' + name + '=([^;]+)'));
    if (match) return match[2];
}
let variableValue = getCookie('variableName');
Each of these methods has its own use case and security implications, so you can choose the one that best fits your needs. If you have any specific requirements or questions about these methods, feel free to ask!
